# Classes, objects, identity, state, and behavior

## Model behavior and invariants
A class defines a type; an instance has identity, state, and behavior. An account's balance is not merely a public number: withdrawals must preserve a nonnegative balance and deposits must be valid. Put those rules with the state they govern.
```java
final class Account {
    private long cents;
    Account(long opening) {
        if (opening < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
        cents = opening;
    }
    void withdraw(long amount) {
        if (amount <= 0 || amount > cents)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("invalid withdrawal");
        cents -= amount;
    }
    long balance() { return cents; }
}
```
Two accounts with the same balance may still represent different accounts. That is entity identity, unlike two measurements representing the same value. Decide which distinction your domain needs.

## Collaboration
A receipt can ask an account for a balance but should not reach into its fields. A transfer use case coordinates two accounts; it also needs an atomicity policy so one side cannot succeed while the other fails. That becomes a transaction concern in later chapters.

## Practice
Create two accounts, withdraw from one, and show the other is unchanged. Try zero, negative, equal-to-balance, and excessive withdrawals. Verify failed calls preserve the old balance. Add deposit with checked arithmetic. Explain why a public setBalance method lets callers bypass the withdrawal contract.

# Constructors, factories, validation, and valid object state

## Construction establishes validity
If every method assumes a nonblank customer name, the constructor must enforce it. A partially valid object shifts checks onto every caller.
```java
final class Customer {
    private final String name;
    Customer(String name) {
        if (name == null || name.isBlank())
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("name required");
        this.name = name.trim();
    }
    String name() { return name; }
}
```
this.name refers to the field; name refers to the parameter. Constructor chaining through this(...) centralizes validation. In Java 21, an explicit this(...) or super(...) constructor invocation must come first.

## Factories
A static factory can express meaning better than overloads: Money.ofCents(500) and Money.ofMajorUnits("5.00") state units. A factory may return a cached instance or an interface implementation. A constructor always creates the declared concrete type. Neither mechanism excuses validation.

Do not call overridable methods from constructors: subclass state may not yet be initialized. Do not register this in a listener or global collection before construction completes.

## Practice
Create a Temperature value with factories for Celsius and Fahrenheit. Store one canonical unit and reject physically impossible values according to a documented policy. Test conversion boundaries and rounding expectations. Add a second constructor only if it represents the same unambiguous concept. Explain what happens when validation throws: the caller receives no successfully constructed value.

# Encapsulation, access modifiers, and information hiding

## Access is a design boundary
private restricts a member to its declaring class's access context. Package-private is the default and allows collaboration within a package. public exposes an API to outside callers. protected supports subclasses and package access; across packages its subclass access rules are narrower than “any protected member anywhere.”

## Hide representation behind behavior
```java
final class Inventory {
    private int available;
    Inventory(int initial) {
        if (initial < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
        available = initial;
    }
    boolean reserve(int quantity) {
        if (quantity <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
        if (quantity > available) return false;
        available -= quantity;
        return true;
    }
}
```
reserve makes the validation and mutation one operation. A getter followed by an outside setter spreads the invariant and later becomes a concurrency hazard. Getters and setters for every field do not establish encapsulation by themselves.

## API evolution
If callers depend only on reserve, the implementation can later use a different representation. If callers directly manipulate available, changing storage also changes their code. Exposing a mutable collection through a getter similarly exposes representation despite private fields.

## Practice
Implement release with positive-quantity validation and overflow checking. Test that failed reservations preserve stock. Sketch a read-only stock query without exposing mutation. Mark which types and members must be public when Inventory moves into its own package. Explain what evidence would justify expanding visibility.

# Static members, dependency injection, and object collaboration

## Static and instance members
A static field belongs to the loaded class; an instance field belongs to an individual object. A static method has no this reference. Constants and stateless utilities often fit static well, but mutable static state creates hidden dependencies between tests and callers.

## Inject collaborators
```java
interface ReceiptSink { void write(String text); }
final class Checkout {
    private final ReceiptSink sink;
    Checkout(ReceiptSink sink) {
        this.sink = java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(sink);
    }
    void finish(long cents) {
        sink.write("Paid " + cents);
    }
}
```
The interface previews the next chapters: it describes an operation a collaborator must provide. Checkout receives that collaborator through its constructor. A console implementation can print; a test implementation can record the text. No framework is required for dependency injection.

## Lifetime matters
The composition root, often main, constructs long-lived collaborators and connects them. If a collaborator owns a file or executor, the application must also decide who closes it. Injecting an object does not transfer ownership automatically. Avoid a global service locator that hides which dependencies a class needs.

## Practice
Use two Checkout instances with different sinks and show they do not share captured receipts. Replace a static mutable total with instance state. Explain why a static final List can still be mutable and why a shared test fixture can make tests pass or fail depending on execution order.

